  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=\u27e82,\\,\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}\u27e9,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=\u27e82,\\,\\frac{3}{4}\u27e9$ when $t=2,$ calculate the time at which the particle is stationary. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe particle is stationary when $t=$\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \u27e82,2{t}^{-3}\u27e9\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e8\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 2{t}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82t+{C}_{1},\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+{C}_{2}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(2)=\u27e82,\\,\\frac{3}{4}\u27e9.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =\u27e82(2)+{C}_{1},\\,-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}+{C}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e82,\\,\\frac{3}{4}\u27e9 & =\u27e84+{C}_{1},\\,-\\frac{1}{4}+{C}_{2}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have ${C}_{1}=-2$ and ${C}_{2}=1.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\u27e82t-2,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ \u27e82t-2,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1\u27e9 & =\u27e80,0\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t-2=0 & \\,\u21d2\\,t=1, \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1=0 & \\,\u21d2\\,t=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution is $t=1.$",
